is currently safe, rather than advertising a submission the prompt will reject.” Pair with: Enter dispatch receipt. S02-09-confirm-overlay State: destructive confirmation with underlying footer visibly blocked. Caption: “Confirmation takes exclusive action ownership instead of leaving the covered surface live.” Pair with: blocked underlying-action receipt. Never use a still image to claim: keyboard correctness focus ownership native child attachment close restoration animation calibration auto-submit absence delayed callback correctness cancellation truthfulness 10. Apply-ready shared article and ledger hunks Record these in 08-article-integration.md; do not edit shared files. Article section Exact heading: Markdown## Core surfaces: typed consistency meets runtime uncertainty Opening claim text after evidence promotion: MarkdownAcross the launcher, Actions, built-ins, prompts, Flow, Notes, Settings, and Agent Chat entry paths, the branch moves more visible behavior into typed descriptors and shared renderers. That is a real maintainability gain where the same descriptor controls the label, shortcut, availability, destination, and execution path. But the audit also found an important limit: several of the strongest-sounding guarantees stop at source text, counts, or model-level tests. Focus transfer, duplicate context staging, native footer ownership, lifecycle cleanup, and shortcut routing still need evidence from the actual GPUI and AppKit boundaries. Use only if the inventory establishes the relative change from main; otherwise prefix the first sentence with The current branch appears to. Evidence links: .notes/oracle/branch-trust-audit/lanes/02-core-surfaces/01-inventory.tsv .notes/oracle/branch-trust-audit/lanes/02-core-surfaces/03-observed-improvements.md .notes/oracle/branch-trust-audit/lanes/02-core-surfaces/04-breakage-candidates.md .notes/oracle/branch-trust-audit/lanes/02-core-surfaces/06-verification-results.md Improvement paragraph hunk Markdown### What genuinely improved The strongest improvements were the ones with a single typed owner: disabled Actions could be rejected by the activation model rather than merely painted gray; clean Agent Chat entry had an explicit no-context policy; Flow and Chat could share conversation-command vocabulary; and conversation turns gained an identity that does not change when the assistant reply arrives. These are meaningful only where the audit traced the descriptor through its renderer, keyboard path, and executor. Confidence label: R3 after focused behavior tests R4 only for items also crossed through runtime automation Risk paragraph hunk Markdown### Where confidence stopped The branch also contains tests that read formatted source and assert that a particular helper call or literal still exists. Those tests can preserve an implementation shape while missing the user-visible failure. More subtly, Agent Chat entry completion is inferred from message and context counts, which cannot prove that the observed change belongs to the initiating request. The audit therefore treats these as proof gaps, not successful behavioral verification. Defect paragraph hunk, conditional Markdown### A small verb exposed a larger contract problem Settings introduced a descriptor intended to drive the footer, semantic projection, accessibility projection, and execution routes from one source. Yet that source assigned the verb “Open” to every row, including operations that clear history, check permissions, request access, and reset window positions. The descriptor achieved consistency, but consistently described several actions incorrectly. This is exactly why shared models must encode meaning, not merely shared formatting. Use only after branch UI or semantic projection reproduces the mismatch. Screenshot integration hunk image: .artifacts/branch-trust-audit/02-core-surfaces/S02-01-actions-root.png caption: The Actions popup now speaks the launcher’s visual language, but focus and shortcut correctness require a separate runtime receipt. confidence: R2 screenshot / R4 paired runtime evidence: receipts/runtime-actions-focus.jsonl Ledger record schema JSON{ "lane": "02-core-surfaces", "findingId": "REG-02-NNN", "verdict": "REPRODUCED_REGRESSION", "confidence": "R4", "severity": "P1", "paths": ["src/path.rs"], "symbols": ["exact_symbol"], "trigger": "concrete input/state", "expected": "expected output/state", "observed": "observed output/state", "evidence": [ ".notes/oracle/branch-trust-audit/lanes/02-core-surfaces/receipts/..." ], "screenshot": ".artifacts/branch-trust-audit/02-core-surfaces/...", "nextStepId": "CORE-NEXT-NN" } 11. Largest article contribution: executable next-step cards Write these fully in 09-prioritized-next-actions.md. Update priority only from evidence. CORE-NEXT-01 — Correlate Agent Chat entry completion Priority: P0 if false acceptance/refusal reproduces; otherwise P1 proof gap Problem: Completion is inferred from aggregate counts rather than request identity. Future production targets: - src/app_impl/agent_handoff/agent_chat_entry.rs - resolved AgentChatStateSnapshot owner Change: 1. Carry request/turn identity through entry dispatch. 2. Expose accepted request refs from the same state source used by the renderer. 3. Expose staged context semantic IDs or direct stage receipts. 4. Resolve completion by identity, not count deltas. Required proof: - duplicate context - concurrent unrelated message - delayed accepted submission - reused and fresh threads - timeout genuinely lacking acceptance Stop condition: No outcome says Accepted, Refused, or Timed Out without a correlated receipt. Forbidden shortcut: Increasing the five-second timeout. CORE-NEXT-02 — Make Actions focus/lifecycle an explicit state machine Priority: P0 if flash-close/orphan reproduces; otherwise P1 Problem: Parent focus, popup active state, deferred focus, singleton storage, automation registration, and native child attachment are coordinated through timing-sensitive booleans. Future production targets: - src/actions/window.rs - src/app_impl/actions_dialog.rs - src/app_impl/actions_toggle.rs Change: 1. Introduce Opening → FocusRequested → Active → Closing → Closed. 2. Block auto-close during Opening/FocusRequested. 3. Centralize unregister/handle removal in one idempotent close transition. 4. Preserve parent identity for Main and Notes. Required proof: - first-open focus - Esc/Cmd+K close - focus-loss close - 20 rapid toggles - Notes-hosted return focus - no runtime or automation handle after close Motion constraint: No timing, geometry, curve, alpha, or fixture changes. CORE-NEXT-03 — Give Settings operations truthful verbs Priority: P1 if visible/AX mismatch reproduces; otherwise P2 Problem: One shared descriptor emits “Open” for clear/check/request/reset operations. Future production targets: - src/render_builtins/settings_contract.rs - any renderer/executor found not to consume the descriptor Change: 1. Model primary verb per action or typed operation. 2. Keep footer, semantic, AX, and execution projections on the same descriptor. 3. Replace universal-open test with per-action semantic cases. Required proof: Clear, Check, Request, Reset, and Open each render and execute truthfully. CORE-NEXT-04 — Retire redundant source audits Priority: P1 governance/proof quality Problem: Exact formatted-source tests duplicate behavior seams and imply confidence they cannot provide. Targets: - src/actions/tests/dialog_runtime_path_tests.rs - src/render_builtins/actions.rs - src/actions/window.rs Change: 1. Delete source test duplicated by entity behavior test. 2. Extract pure typed config resolvers where needed. 3. Use compiler-visible references for existence contracts. 4. Use placement receipt behavior tests for geometry. 5. Use runtime automation for registration, child attachment, and focus order. Required proof: Behavior remains covered while owned source-reader sites decrease. Do not: Weaken the global source-audit guard or add replacement source reads elsewhere. CORE-NEXT-05 — Establish one executable shortcut-parity census Priority: P1 Problem: Badges, key resolvers, native footer dispatch, Actions shortcuts, and surface handlers can drift independently. Targets: - action/command descriptor owners - src/app_impl/ui_window.rs - src/app_impl/simulate_key_dispatch.rs - prompt/chat key resolvers Change: 1. Export typed advertised-command descriptors. 2. For each enabled badge, declare one owner. 3. Test resolver output from that descriptor. 4. Runtime-probe representative native-window and prompt paths. Required proof: No enabled visible badge lacks an owner; disabled badges cannot execute; plain Cmd+C remains native selection copy where appropriate. Avoid: A new source-string census. CORE-NEXT-06 — Prove stale asynchronous work cannot change the current surface Priority: P1 if stale resize/result reproduction exists; otherwise P2 Problem: filtering, refresh, handoff completion, window resizing, and startup callbacks cross deferred/asynchronous boundaries. Targets: - filter_input_change.rs / filter_input_updates.rs - lifecycle_reset.rs - ui_window.rs - built-in async loaders - Day Page round-trip code Change: 1. Bind each callback to view identity plus generation. 2. Ignore stale completions before mutating selection, data, footer, or size. 3. Log a structured stale-drop receipt. Required proof: Switch surfaces during an in-flight load/resize/handoff and show the new surface remains unchanged. 12. Final accounting and stop gate Run this lane-local completion check: Bashpython3 - "$LANE" <<'PY' import csv import pathlib import sys lane = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1]) scope = lane / "00-scope.tsv" inventory = lane / "01-inventory.tsv" with scope.open(encoding="utf-8") as f: expected_rows = list(csv.DictReader(f, delimiter="\t")) with inventory.open(encoding="utf-8") as f: actual_rows = list(csv.DictReader(f, delimiter="\t")) expected = [row["path"] for row in expected_rows] actual = [row["path"] for row in actual_rows] assert len(expected) == 128, len(expected) assert len(set(expected)) == 128 assert len(actual) == 128, len(actual) assert set(actual) == set(expected), ( sorted(set(expected) - set(actual)), sorted(set(actual) - set(expected)), ) allowed_states = { "DIFFED", "TRACED", "PROVED", "ACCOUNTED_UNVERIFIED", } allowed_verdicts = { "VERIFIED_IMPROVEMENT", "STATIC_IMPROVEMENT", "REPRODUCED_REGRESSION", "PLAUSIBLE_REGRESSION", "UNPROVEN_CLAIM", "PROOF_DEBT", "NO_MATERIAL_CHANGE", } allowed_confidence = {"R0", "R1", "R2", "R3", "R4"} for row in actual_rows: assert row["review_state"] in allowed_states, row assert row["verdict"] in allowed_verdicts, row assert row["confidence"] in allowed_confidence, row assert row["evidence"].strip(), row print("PASS: 128/128 owned paths accounted with verdict, confidence, and evidence") PY The package is complete only when: all 128 paths appear exactly once in 01-inventory.tsv; every changed production symbol has been classified through its subsystem; every material claim has a receipt or explicit UNPROVEN_CLAIM; focused checks have immutable first-run logs and, where applicable, one rerun log; keyboard, focus, lifecycle, temporal, native-window, and AI-entry claims are not “proved” by screenshots; motion checks are green without fixture or value changes; screenshot requests have exact states, captions, and evidence limitations; article and ledger hunks are complete; next-step cards are prioritized and executable; 10-worker-report.md names all skipped verification and why; no production file, generated output, shared article, root ledger, manifest, commit, push, merge, app deployment, or publication was changed. Excluded optional hardening Do not add new frameworks, redesign adjacent surfaces, split monoliths merely for cleanliness, retune glass, weaken tests, create a duplicate command registry, run an unbounded whole-repository suite, or remediate production defects during this campaign. Record supported future changes in the lane